CAT = "cmaIntake"; SEG = "INT"
Q = [
("While taking a manual blood pressure, the medical assistant hears the first clear tapping sound as the cuff deflates. What does this sound mark?",
 "The systolic pressure", ["The diastolic pressure", "The pulse pressure", "The mean arterial pressure"],
 "The first clear Korotkoff sound (phase I) marks the systolic pressure. The point where the sounds disappear (phase V) is recorded as the diastolic pressure in adults."),
("A patient's blood pressure is 142/88 mm Hg. What is the pulse pressure?",
 "54 mm Hg", ["88 mm Hg", "230 mm Hg", "142 mm Hg"],
 "Pulse pressure is systolic minus diastolic: 142 - 88 = 54 mm Hg. Adding the two numbers gives 230, which has no clinical meaning."),
("A provider orders a blood pressure taken on the thigh because both arms are in casts. Over which artery is the stethoscope placed?",
 "Popliteal artery", ["Femoral artery", "Dorsalis pedis artery", "Posterior tibial artery"],
 "A thigh pressure uses a large cuff on the thigh with the stethoscope over the popliteal artery behind the knee. Thigh readings usually run higher than arm readings."),
("Two staff count a patient's pulse at the same time: 96 at the apex and 82 at the radial artery. What is the pulse deficit?",
 "14 beats per minute", ["82 beats per minute", "178 beats per minute", "7 beats per minute"],
 "Pulse deficit is apical minus radial: 96 - 82 = 14. A deficit means some heartbeats are too weak to reach the wrist and should be reported."),
("An older patient's breathing cycles from deep, rapid breaths to shallow breaths and then short pauses with no breathing. How is this pattern documented?",
 "Cheyne-Stokes respirations", ["Kussmaul respirations", "Tachypnea", "Hyperventilation"],
 "Cheyne-Stokes breathing waxes and wanes with periods of apnea and is seen in heart failure and near death. Kussmaul breathing is steadily deep and rapid, as in ketoacidosis."),
("How is the length of a 9-month-old infant measured at a well-child visit?",
 "Lying flat on a measuring board", ["Standing straight against a wall chart", "Held upright on a parent's lap", "Estimated from the last visit"],
 "Children under about 2 years are measured recumbent, crown to heel, with the legs gently straightened. Standing height is used once the child can stand steadily."),
("At a well-baby visit, where is the tape measure placed to measure head circumference?",
 "Above the eyebrows and ears", ["Across the top of the head", "Around the jaw and the crown of the head", "Below the ears at the neck"],
 "Head circumference is taken around the widest part of the head: just above the eyebrows and ears in front and over the most prominent part of the back of the head."),
("A 3-year-old's weight plots at the 75th percentile on the growth chart. What does this mean?",
 "Heavier than 75% of peers", ["Lighter than 75% of peers", "75% of an ideal weight", "Overweight for her height"],
 "A percentile compares the child with children of the same age and sex: at the 75th, the child weighs more than 75 of every 100. Trends over time matter more than one plot."),
("A surgical report lists a tracheotomy. What does the suffix \"-otomy\" mean?",
 "Cutting into", ["Surgical removal", "Making a permanent opening", "Surgical repair"],
 "\"-otomy\" means incision into. \"-ectomy\" is removal, \"-ostomy\" creates an opening to the outside, and \"-plasty\" is surgical repair."),
("A patient's problem list includes nephrolithiasis. What is this condition?",
 "Kidney stones", ["Gallstones", "Kidney inflammation", "Bladder infection"],
 "\"Nephr/o\" means kidney and \"lith\" means stone. Gallstones are cholelithiasis; kidney inflammation is nephritis."),
("A patient complains of arthralgia. What is the patient describing?",
 "Joint pain", ["Muscle weakness", "Bone thinning", "Swollen tendons"],
 "\"Arthr/o\" is joint and \"-algia\" is pain. Bone thinning is osteoporosis; muscle weakness is myasthenia."),
("A referral is for rhinoplasty. What will the surgeon do?",
 "Reshape or repair the nose", ["Remove the tonsils", "View the nasal passages with a scope", "Drain the sinuses"],
 "\"Rhin/o\" is nose and \"-plasty\" is surgical repair. Viewing the nasal passages would be rhinoscopy."),
("During intake, the medical assistant needs to know whether the patient is allergic to latex. Which type of question fits best?",
 "A closed, direct question", ["An open-ended, exploratory question", "A leading question", "A reflective statement"],
 "Closed questions get a short, specific answer such as yes or no, which suits allergy checks. Open-ended questions suit the history of a complaint."),
("Which question asked during intake is a leading question that should be avoided?",
 "\"You take all your pills, right?\"", ["\"Which pills do you take each day?\"", "\"How often do you miss a dose?\"", "\"Do you take any vitamins?\""],
 "A leading question suggests the expected answer, so patients tend to agree rather than report what they actually do. Neutral wording gets more accurate answers."),
("Which entry in the visit note is objective data?",
 "Temperature 101.3 F orally", ["Reports chills since Sunday", "States throat feels raw", "Says she is very tired"],
 "Objective data is measured or observed by staff, such as a temperature. What the patient reports, such as chills or tiredness, is subjective."),
("Which information belongs in a patient's social and occupational history?",
 "Tobacco use and job exposures", ["Mother's breast cancer", "Childhood surgeries", "Current symptoms by body system"],
 "Social history covers habits (tobacco, alcohol, drugs), occupation, exposures and living situation. A parent's illness is family history; past surgeries are past medical history."),
("A patient tells the medical assistant that her father had a heart attack at 50. Where is this recorded?",
 "Family history", ["Past medical history", "Review of systems", "History of present illness"],
 "Illnesses of blood relatives go in the family history because they point to inherited risk. The patient's own past illnesses go in past medical history."),
("During intake the medical assistant asks a series of questions about each body system, from head to toe. What is this part of the history called?",
 "Review of systems", ["Chief complaint", "Physical examination", "Past medical history"],
 "The review of systems is a structured list of symptoms the patient reports for each body system. The physical examination is the provider's hands-on, objective assessment."),
("A medical assistant finds a wrong pulse value written in a paper chart. How is it corrected?",
 "One line through, initials and date", ["Correction fluid, then rewrite", "Black out the old entry with a marker", "Tear out and rewrite the page"],
 "A single line keeps the original entry readable, and the correction is added with initials and date. Erasing, obliterating or removing entries can look like tampering with a legal record."),
("Which screening tool is commonly used in the office to screen adults for depression?",
 "PHQ-9 questionnaire", ["Snellen chart", "Glasgow Coma Scale score", "Apgar score"],
 "The PHQ-9 asks nine questions about mood over the past two weeks and scores severity. The Apgar score rates newborns at birth."),
("A provider orders osteoporosis screening for a 67-year-old woman. Which test is used?",
 "DEXA bone density scan", ["Serum calcium level", "Plain X-ray of the spine", "Thyroid ultrasound"],
 "Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) measures bone mineral density at the hip and spine and gives a T-score. Blood calcium is normal in most osteoporosis."),
("Using the 5 A's model for tobacco cessation, what is the first step at every visit?",
 "Ask about tobacco use", ["Advise the patient to quit", "Arrange follow-up visits", "Assist with a quit plan"],
 "The 5 A's are Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist and Arrange. Asking and recording tobacco status at every visit comes first."),
("The office screens every adult for intimate partner violence. How should the question be asked?",
 "Privately, without the partner", ["In front of family members", "Only if bruises are seen", "On a form handed to the partner"],
 "Screening is done when the patient is alone so that an abuser cannot hear the answer. Asking every patient avoids profiling and missed cases."),
("During intake a patient says, \"I have a plan to end my life tonight.\" What should the medical assistant do?",
 "Stay and alert the provider now", ["Schedule a counseling visit", "Give a crisis hotline card", "Finish the intake, then report it later"],
 "A stated plan is an emergency. The patient is not left alone, and the provider is notified at once so that immediate safety steps can be taken."),
("A patient stands with new axillary crutches and grips the handpieces. How should the elbows look if the handgrips are at the right height?",
 "Bent about 20 to 30 degrees", ["Locked fully straight", "Bent to a right angle", "Raised above the shoulders"],
 "The handgrips are set so the elbows flex about 20 to 30 degrees, letting the arms push down and lift the body. Straight elbows cannot take the weight, and a right angle means the grips are set too high."),
("A patient with a weak right leg is learning to use a walker. Which sequence should the medical assistant reinforce?",
 "Walker, weak leg, strong leg", ["Strong leg, weak leg, walker", "Weak leg, walker, strong leg", "Both legs, then the walker"],
 "The walker is lifted or rolled a short step ahead, then the weaker leg steps into it, followed by the stronger leg. Stepping before moving the walker risks a fall."),
("A provider asks the medical assistant to reinforce diet teaching for a patient with chronic kidney disease. Which nutrients are commonly limited?",
 "Potassium and phosphorus", ["Fiber and vitamin C", "Calcium and vitamin D supplements", "Iron and folic acid"],
 "Damaged kidneys cannot clear potassium and phosphorus well, so both build up in the blood. Sodium and sometimes protein are also limited, as the provider orders."),
("Which vitamins are fat-soluble and can build up in the body if taken in excess?",
 "A, D, E and K", ["B1, B6 and B12", "C and folic acid", "Niacin and biotin"],
 "Vitamins A, D, E and K are stored in fat and the liver, so large supplement doses can become toxic. The B vitamins and vitamin C are water-soluble and excess is excreted."),
]
